  "text": "To find the sum of all integer bases \\( b > 9 \\) for which \\( 17_b \\) is a divisor of \\( 97_b \\), we first need to convert these numbers from base \\( b \\) to base 10. \n\nFor \\( 17_b \\):\n\\[ 17_b = 1 \\cdot b + 7 = b + 7 \\]\n\nFor \\( 97_b \\):\n\\[ 97_b = 9 \\cdot b + 7 = 9b + 7 \\]\n\nWe need \\( b + 7 \\) to be a divisor of \\( 9b + 7 \\). Let's denote \\( d = b + 7 \\). Then \\( d \\) must divide \\( 9b + 7 \\). \n\nSubstitute \\( d = b + 7 \\) into \\( 9b + 7 \\):\n\\[ 9b + 7 = 9(b + 7) - 56 = 9d - 56 \\]\n\nSo, \\( d \\) must divide \\( 9d - 56 \\). This implies that \\( d \\) must also divide 56. The integer divisors of 56 are:\n\\[ \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4, \\pm 7, \\pm 8, \\pm 14, \\pm 28, \\pm 56 \\]\n\nSince \\( b > 9 \\), \\( d = b + 7 \\) must be greater than 16. Therefore, we only consider the positive divisors greater than 16:\n\\[ d \\in \\{28, 56\\} \\]\n\nNow, we solve for \\( b \\):\n\\[ b + 7 = 28 \\implies b = 21 \\]\n\\[ b + 7 = 56 \\implies b = 49 \\]\n\nThe sum of these bases is:\n\\[ 21 + 49 = 70 \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{70}\n\\]",
